{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe mcp entrypoint skips cloning the tickets branch whenever `${REBAR_TRACKER_DIR}/.git` merely\nEXISTS. A `.git` directory is not proof of a usable clone, and the named volume PERSISTS across\ncontainers — so one interrupted clone poisons the store permanently. No later container\nre-clones, and the server serves an empty tracker forever.\n\n## Observed in production (2026-08-26)\n\nThe 120s blue-green health rollback removed containers mid-clone, repeatedly (that was bug\n`unfit-beneficial-whimbrel`). Each removal left the volume holding orphaned objects. The\nsurviving state:\n\n    du -sh $REBAR_TRACKER_DIR   ->  1.5G\n    ls $REBAR_TRACKER_DIR       ->  0 ticket directories\n    cat .git/HEAD               ->  ref: refs/heads/.invalid\n    ls .git/refs/heads          ->  (empty)\n    git rev-parse HEAD          ->  fatal: your current branch appears to be broken\n    rebar list (in container)   ->  []\n\n1.5 GB of objects, no worktree, no resolvable HEAD. Every subsequent container saw `.git`,\nskipped the clone, and served `[]`.\n\n## Why this is worse than a missing store\n\nThe absent-store guard (`antelopine-limivorous-chinchilla`) makes a MISSING store loud —\n`store_uninitialized`. A poisoned store reads as PRESENT, so the guard correctly does not fire\nand reads return `[]` again. The silent-empty failure mode is reintroduced through a different\ndoor: a store that exists, is empty, and has no connection to origin.\n\n## Fix\n\nRequire a RESOLVABLE HEAD before trusting the clone, and clear the directory before re-cloning\nso partial objects cannot poison the retry:\n\n    if ! git -C \"$DIR\" rev-parse --verify -q HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n      rm -rf \"$DIR\"/.[!.]* \"$DIR\"/*\n      git clone --single-branch --branch tickets \"$URL\" \"$DIR\" || echo \"clone deferred\" >&2\n    fi\n\nVerified against the exact production shape (`.git` present, HEAD -> refs/heads/.invalid, empty\nrefs/heads): the old guard SKIPS, the new one re-clones. A healthy store still resolves HEAD and\nis skipped, so no needless re-clone of a ~200k-commit branch.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A tracker dir whose HEAD does not resolve is treated as unusable and re-cloned, not\n      trusted because `.git` exists.\n- [ ] The directory is cleared BEFORE the re-clone, so partial objects cannot survive into it.\n- [ ] A healthy store (resolvable HEAD) is still skipped — no re-clone on every boot.\n- [ ] Regression coverage pins the verify -> clear -> clone ORDER; clearing after the clone\n      would delete the fresh store.\n\n## Verification\n\n    .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py -q -p no:randomly\n\nTeeth: reverting the guard to the `.git`-existence check turns\n`test_mcp_entrypoint_reclones_an_unusable_store_rather_than_trusting_dot_git` RED.\n\n## Scope growth recorded during implementation (2026-08-26)\n\nCode review on patchsets 1-2 expanded this fix in two ways. Both are recorded here so the plan\nmatches the change rather than the change outrunning the plan.\n\n### 1. Extract the entrypoint into a real script file\n\nThe entrypoint was generated inside a `RUN { echo '...'; ... } > /usr/local/bin/mcp-entrypoint.sh`\nblock, so the only possible test was a substring/order grep over the Dockerfile SOURCE — a\nchange-detector that cannot execute the verify -> clear -> clone path it claims to pin. Review\ncalled this out as blocking: \"No test drives the actual re-clone behavior.\"\n\nThe script moves to `infra/scripts/mcp-entrypoint.sh`, COPYed and installed by the Dockerfile,\nfollowing the precedent already set by `infra/scripts/reviewbot-ensure-tickets.sh`. Behavior is\npreserved exactly; the point is that `provision_store` becomes directly executable against a\ntemp dir with a stubbed `git`, so the acceptance criteria above can be pinned by RUNNING them.\n\n### 2. Serialize the re-clone across containers sharing the volume\n\nProvisioning is backgrounded (`provision_store &` then `exec \"$@\"`) precisely because the\n~200k-commit clone outruns the 120s readiness deadline. The deploy flock therefore serializes\ndeploy TICKS but NOT provisioning windows: `/health` answers before the store exists, autodeploy\ndeclares success and releases the lock mid-clone, and the 2-minute timer can start a second\ncontainer into the same volume. That container sees an unresolvable HEAD — because the first is\nstill cloning — and clears the directory out from under it. That is this ticket's own poisoning\nmechanism, reachable through the ordinary path.\n\nTwo further lock-free launch paths exist: the compose `mcp:` service carries `restart: always`\non the same volume, and the client-PAT runbook prescribes a manual restart.\n\nSo the re-clone takes a lock. Acquisition must be atomic WITH its timestamp (a hard-linked\nfile written before it is linked into place), because a lock that exists briefly without a\nreadable marker is either a race (peers read it as stale and break it) or, if missing markers\nare treated as fresh, a permanent wedge — the exact failure class this ticket exists to remove.\n\n## Additional acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `provision_store` is executable in a test against a temp dir with a stubbed `git`; the\n      poisoned, healthy-skip, and clone-failure paths are each driven by RUNNING the script,\n      not by grepping its source.\n- [ ] The `rm -rf` clear refuses a `REBAR_TRACKER_DIR` that is empty, `/`, or relative, and a\n      second layer makes a bypassed guard inert rather than catastrophic.\n- [ ] Two containers whose provisioning windows overlap cannot both clear-and-clone the shared\n      volume; the loser observes the winner's completed clone instead of re-cloning.\n- [ ] Lock acquisition is atomic with its timestamp: there is no window in which the lock\n      exists without a readable acquired-at, so no peer can misread a fresh lock as stale.\n- [ ] A container that dies holding the lock does not wedge the store permanently."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787773028234698001,"uuid":"9f320fda-0521-4d77-9131-9b657aae2a14"}